          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Louisiana Purchase</strong><br>\nThe Louisiana Purchase was a landmark achievement of Jefferson's presidency. In 1803, he negotiated a deal with France to buy the Louisiana Territory, which doubled the size of the United States. The purchase demonstrated his belief in westward expansion, although it conflicted with his constitutional principles.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Lewis and Clark Expedition</strong><br>\nJefferson was instrumental in launching the Lewis and Clark Expedition. He tasked Meriwether Lewis and William Clark to explore the newly acquired western territory. Their journey, from 1804 to 1806, provided valuable information about the geography, biology, and potential for expansion in the west.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Jefferson's Views on Slavery</strong><br>\nWhile Jefferson publicly condemned slavery, he was also a slave owner. He saw slavery as a necessary evil and advocated for its end, but he did not free all of his slaves during his lifetime. This paradox is a significant aspect of his historical legacy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
