            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Educational Contributions</strong><br>\nJefferson's contributions to education were significant. He founded the University of Virginia, emphasizing the importance of education for democracy. He believed that an informed citizenry was essential to the health of the new nation and advocated for educational reform.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Retirement and Final Years</strong><br>\nAfter serving two terms as president, Jefferson retired to his home, Monticello, in Virginia. He spent his last years engaged in educational pursuits, correspondence, and managing his estate. He continued to mentor young politicians and remained active in political discourse until his death.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nThomas Jefferson died on July 4, 1826, the 50th anniversary of the Declaration of Independence. He is remembered as a Founding Father, the author of the Declaration of Independence, and a proponent of democracy. His contributions to the early United States have</p>\n"
              },
